# G063 — Josephus

*v2. See `josephus.md` for prose: classic recurrence, O(n) iteration vs
O(n²) simulation, survivor position under round-robin elimination.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Round-Robin with Skips

[fair-shift-assignment @team @current-shift @counter @k
    IN ORDER TO
        distribute an unpleasant shift across the team fairly
        by cycling the roster and skipping @k-1 members each time
        so the assignment has no "always me" bias

    @team
        where length is at least 1

    @victim-idx <- (@counter + @k - 1) mod @team:length
    @victim     <- @team at-index @victim-idx

    [scheduler[assign-shift {agent: @victim, shift: @current-shift}]]
    [state[set {key: "shift-counter",
                 value: (@counter + @k) mod @team:length}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[josephus
    survivor        {n, k}                  ;; O(n) via recurrence
    simulate        {n, k}                  ;; (survivor, elimination-order)
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: recurrence as `where`-expressed contract

The v1 surfaced the O(n) recurrence:

[josephus[survivor {n: @n, k: @k}]
    where
        survivor(1, k) = 0
        and survivor(n, k) = (survivor(n-1, k) + k) mod n
        and result is in 0..@n (zero-indexed)
]

The resolver satisfies the contract however it likes. The calling
choreography trusts the recurrence.

---

## Simulation includes elimination order

[josephus[simulate {n: @n, k: @k}]
    where
        second return value is the full elimination order
        starting from the first eliminated and ending just before the survivor
        useful for "show the stages of the problem" tutorials or visualisations
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [josephus[survivor {n: 41, k: 3}]]
    ;; -> 30  (the classic Josephus problem)

    -> [josephus[survivor {n: 7, k: 2}]]
    ;; -> 6  (2^n - 1 pattern for k=2)

    @(survivor, order) <- [josephus[simulate {n: 5, k: 2}]]
    -> @survivor                                         ;; -> 2
    -> @order                                             ;; -> [1, 3, 0, 4] (elim order)
]
